    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d756">

                <pb facs="0724" n="716" sameAs="frus:frus1941v03/pg716"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/11606: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Beirut</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Engert</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Beirut</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-06-03T11:00:00+02:00">June 3, 1941—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received 3:10 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>184. Personal for the Under Secretary. May I suggest that you impress upon the
                    British great importance especially at this time of not broadcasting unconfirmed
                    reports regarding Syria. I refer for instance to the recent announcement that
                    400 Germans had arrived on the French hospital ship (see my 178, May 30<anchor
                        n="74" xml:id="d756-f1"/>) or that German troops had landed at Latakia—refer
                    my 181, May 31. As both French and natives listen eagerly to British broadcasts
                    the present great confidence in their general accuracy will be undermined if
                    they hear statements which they know to be incorrect.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Engert</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
